                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "NQ3Dk",
                      "name": "Prose",
                      "fill": "#5c564f",
                      "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                      "width": "fill_container",
                      "content": "The student wants to understand stereochemical inversion. I should explain the linear trigonal bipyramidal transition state in the concerted SN2 mechanism, use the umbrella inversion analogy, and cite page 142 of Clayden.",
                      "lineHeight": 1.45,
                      "fontFamily": "Lora",
                      "fontSize": 11.5,
                      "fontWeight": "normal",
                      "fontStyle": "italic"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "r5T6C",
                  "name": "tutorMsg",
                  "fill": "#2d2a26",
                  "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "content": "Tutor: The backside attack proceeds via a concerted mechanism. As the nucleophile forms a bond from the side opposite to the leaving group, the transition state exhibits a trigonal bipyramidal structure. Upon completion, the three non-reacting groups are pushed through, resulting in an inversion of configuration, akin to an umbrella turning inside out in a strong wind.",
                  "lineHeight": 1.45,
                  "fontFamily": "Lora",
                  "fontSize": 12,
                  "fontWeight": "normal"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "YKK8h",
                  "name": "citation",
                  "fill": "#3d705c",
                  "content": "[Reference: Ch. 7, p. 142]",
                  "fontFamily": "Plus Jakarta Sans",
                  "fontSize": 10,
                  "fontWeight": "bold"
                }
              ]
